From 1d4aaa8f4b7e626330f67e3ca7ff6916993da189 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HeDo <ldileo@digipa.it>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 19:06:44 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] plylib can bound the length of a list it reads into
 memory the caller allocated

A list property is read into the memory at offset1 for as many items as the file says it
has: one byte in a binary file (up to 255), any int in an ASCII file. A caller that reads a
list into a fixed array had no way to say how big the array is, so a longer list wrote past
it. PropDescriptor gets maxlist (0, the default, keeps the old behaviour): every list read
callback fails the read when the length is negative or above it, after storing the length,
so the caller can say why the read failed. AddToRead takes it as a trailing default
argument, so existing callers compile and behave as before.
---
 wrap/ply/plylib.cpp | 68 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 wrap/ply/plylib.h   | 20 +++++++++----
 2 files changed, 80 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/wrap/ply/plylib.cpp b/wrap/ply/plylib.cpp
index 9ef4d10bc..10803e4e6 100644
--- a/wrap/ply/plylib.cpp
+++ b/wrap/ply/plylib.cpp
@@ -594,6 +594,12 @@ static inline int ReadDoubleA( XFILE * fp, double * d )
 
 
 
+// false when a list of n items does not fit the limit of its descriptor
+static inline bool ListFits( const PropDescriptor * d, int n )
+{
+	return d->maxlist==0 || (n>=0 && size_t(n)<=d->maxlist);
+}
